WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.600
Abraham: Employers who employ cyber
security professionals will almost

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.359
always conduct screening checks.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:10.639
Just thinks about it if you are taking
someone on who is there to look after

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:12.320
your cyber security, wouldn’t you?

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.049
Nicole: Pre-employment or vetting
checks can help an organisation protect

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:21.580
itself from potential negative impact
that could happen if an employee was

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.349
taken on that might not have the best
interests of the company in mind.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.785
There are also some legal requirements
that are necessary such as simple as

00:00:29.785 --> 00:00:34.219
checking a candidate’s legal right to
work in the UK or more seriously whether

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:37.980
a criminal record prevents them from
being employed in a particular role.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.660
Abraham: Most checks that take place are
usually related to the role a candidate

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.680
has applied for or for depending on
the organisation they want to work for.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.190
Medical checks may be required for
a role or for insurance purposes.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.899
Nicole: Criminal records checks
may be relevant when working with

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.810
children or in healthcare roles.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.669
References can also check if a person
has misrepresented their previous roles.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.310
Abraham: Many cyber security organisations
will conduct online and social media

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.370
checks to make sure that their employees’
behaviour, does not go against the

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:14.710
beliefs and ethics of the organisation
or will not harm it’s reputation.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.770
Nicole: Any pre-employment checks done
must always be carried out in accordance

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.209
with data protection guidelines
according to the Data protection

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:29.030
Act 2018, making sure of accuracy,
relevancy, and the appropriate security.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.050
Abraham: Any inaccuracies should
be challenged, and information

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.320
should be kept up to date.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.690
Some checks may also need written
consent from the candidate such as

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.359
with health checks, but all employers
must make sure that they do not

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.030
discriminate by only conducting
checks on certain groups of people.

00:01:46.709 --> 00:01:50.750
So, what are the consequences
of any unsatisfactory findings

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.240
as a result of employer checks?

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.600
Nicole: Consequences of
unsatisfactory findings can include,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.460
not getting a job due to being
unable to get security clearance.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.259
It can also mean a candidate not
being able to work in specific

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:06.690
areas or for high profile employers.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.370
Abraham: These will always depend on
the type of check that takes place.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:15.829
For example, lying on a CV about
previous roles, or not having permission

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:19.310
to work in the UK would likely mean
that a job offer would be withdrawn.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:24.420
Nicole: For work involving finance or
the law or contact with vulnerable people

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.370
any type of unsatisfactory findings, will
usually cost the candidate a job offer.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.319
High profile employers will not hire
those with unsatisfactory findings on any

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.209
checks as they will be concerned about
reputation and security issues and will

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.099
have to follow regulatory guidelines.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:45.780
Abraham: However, saying that there
should also be a requirement on some

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.430
organisations to give an applicant chance
to explain some unsatisfactory findings

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.500
for example something that happened a
long time ago, like not paying a fine

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.839
may cause someone to have a criminal
record, but should not necessarily

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.140
be a barrier to future employment
if it does not impact their role.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.910
Nicole: In cases like this it is
important for applicants to be upfront

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.179
and truthful about any issues that
may arise on their application, so

00:03:11.179 --> 00:03:14.640
as not to have the role snatched away
from them at the end of the process.

